      "title": "How Revenue Leaders Think: The VP of Sales Mental Model",
      "module": "The VP of Sales Operating System",
      "estimatedMinutes": 28,
      "content": "<h2>The VP of Sales Is Not a Senior Sales Rep</h2><p>The single most common reason new VPs of Sales fail is that they were promoted for being an exceptional individual contributor and then continued to think like one. The skills that make someone a top closer, personal hustle, deal instinct, charisma in the room, are necessary but almost entirely irrelevant to the VP role. The VP of Sales does not win deals. The VP of Sales builds a system that wins deals predictably, at scale, without depending on any single person, including themselves.</p><p>This is a mental model shift, not a skill upgrade. The individual contributor optimizes for their own number. The frontline manager optimizes for their team's number this quarter. The VP of Sales optimizes for the revenue machine, the durable, compounding system of people, process, and infrastructure that produces revenue quarter after quarter regardless of who is in any given seat. If your value to the company disappears the moment you stop personally intervening in deals, you are not operating as a VP of Sales. You are operating as an expensive super-rep.</p><h2>The Three Time Horizons</h2><p>Revenue leaders think across three time horizons simultaneously. The first is this quarter: the deals in flight, the forecast, the gaps that need closing now. The second is the next two to four quarters: the pipeline being generated today that converts later, the reps ramping, the process changes taking hold. The third is the next one to three years: the org design, the leadership bench, the systems and culture that determine whether the company can scale from $10M to $50M to $100M.</p><p>Weak VPs live entirely in horizon one. They are perpetually firefighting, sprinting from deal to deal, and they wonder why next quarter always looks exactly as chaotic as this one. The discipline of a true revenue leader is protecting time for horizons two and three even when horizon one is screaming for attention. The quarter will always feel urgent. The system is what makes future quarters not feel that way.</p><h2>Outputs Are Lagging Indicators</h2><p>Revenue is a lagging indicator. By the time the number is bad, the causes are months old. The VP of Sales mental model is obsessed with leading indicators: pipeline created, activity quality, conversion rates by stage, ramp progress, rep engagement. These are the dials you can actually turn. The number is just the readout of dials you turned a quarter ago.</p><p>When a VP says \"we need to close more deals,\" they have said nothing useful. When a VP says \"our stage-two-to-stage-three conversion dropped from 55 percent to 38 percent over six weeks, here is the discovery quality problem causing it, and here is the coaching intervention,\" they are operating the machine. The number is the score. Leading indicators are the game.</p><h2>Leverage: The Core Discipline</h2><p>Every VP of Sales has the same constraint: time. The difference between a VP who scales an organization and one who becomes its bottleneck is how they think about leverage. There are exactly four sources of VP leverage. Hiring: every great hire multiplies output without your involvement. Systems: every process, playbook, and piece of infrastructure that produces a good outcome without you. Coaching: every manager and rep you make permanently better. Decisions: the small number of high-stakes calls, on strategy, comp, segmentation, key hires, that shape everything downstream.</p><p>Notice what is not on that list: closing deals, building decks, attending every call. Those feel productive and are deeply satisfying for a former top rep. They are also, for a VP, almost pure waste. The painful truth of the role is that the work that feels most like \"real selling\" is the work you must most ruthlessly delegate or eliminate.</p><h2>You Are Now a Revenue Executive</h2><p>The final shift is identity. A VP of Sales is a member of the executive team who happens to own the sales function, not the head of sales who occasionally visits the executive team. You will spend significant time with the CEO, the CFO, the board, marketing, product, and customer success. Your forecast is a financial commitment the company plans around. Your headcount requests are capital allocation decisions. The VPs who earn $250K to $500K and the path to CRO are the ones who carry themselves as revenue executives, not as the most senior person in the sales bullpen.</p>",
